 while the defense sits 24th in EPA allowed. What sets them apart is the 2nd highest under center rate in the league.","offense":"The Lions have one of the most efficient offenses in the league so far, ranking in the top eight in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix has stayed close to what game situations call for. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd highest under center rate and the 2nd lowest shotgun rate in the league. Jared Goff has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Amon-Ra St. Brown, who has drawn 30% of the targets.","defense":"The Lions defense has been well below average by EPA allowed per play so far. It has been much better against the run than the pass, ranking 8th and 24th. Aidan Hutchinson has been as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise is Alim McNeill, who has 6.5 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is an eight man box rate that ranks 7th highest in the league.","special":"The Lions have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 57% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 94% of field goals, which is one of the best in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their receivers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Jared Goff has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,437 yards. Amon-Ra St. Brown has been the clear top target with 30% of the looks and 755 receiving yards. Jahmyr Gibbs has handled most of the work in the backfield with 83% of the carries. On defense, Alim McNeill leads the team with 6.5 sacks and Chuck Clark has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Drew Petzing is calling the offense and Kelvin Sheppard is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2023 Raiders. Their defense looks most like the 2022 Cowboys under Dan Quinn and the 2022 Browns.","trends":"The offense went from 7th in EPA per play in 2025 to 6th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 14th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 24th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was pre-snap motion rate, which went from 60% to 54%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}